gm-method (two terrain-consumer lessons). (1) OCCLUSION via shared depth on an MSAA canvas: a backdrop renderer that draws into its OWN FBO and upscales COLOR-only leaves a consumer scene with no depth to occlude against (geometry draws OVER the terrain). The fix is to write the backdrop depth into the canvas depth buffer - but blitFramebuffer(DEPTH) from a single-sample FBO to an MSAA canvas (canvas SAMPLES>1, common) is GL_INVALID_OPERATION and silently fails; use a SHADER PASS instead (full-screen tri, gl_FragDepth = texture(depthTex,uv), colorMask off, depthFunc ALWAYS) - MSAA-safe, per-fragment. Witness the canvas is MSAA via gl.getParameter(gl.SAMPLES). (2) PLACEMENT/collider floating off the DRAWN mesh: the displayed surface is the renderer's quadtree LEAF verts LINEARLY interpolated at the CURRENT distance-LOD, but a fine height sampler (full composeHeight) follows finer detail between those verts -> the gap SCALES with leaf vert spacing (measured: 2m leaf -> 0.05m float, 62m leaf -> ~4m). So placement on the fine surface floats wherever the render draws a coarse leaf. Options: match the render's live-LOD leaf (linear interp of its verts), or FORCE the render finest near the player (raise maxLevel so the near leaf vert spacing ~ the placement spacing) so drawn==placement - the latter costs near-player triangles (quadtree distance-falloff keeps far cheap). maxLevel must be passed to the render init AND the placement/collider sampler so both agree. GENERAL: 'matches the verts' means matching the rendered LINEAR-interp-between-LOD-verts surface, not the true fine field.